// scripts/** is excluded from the main tsconfig, so `tsc --noEmit` reports a
// clean tree while every script that writes to the production database goes
// unchecked. On 2026-08-30 that let two broken scripts ship in consecutive
// commits — an undefined identifier in enrich-repos.ts (which writes repo
// grades to prod) and a missing import in an eval probe — with vitest, the
// main tsc, and a full CI run all green.
//
// This config type-checks them. It cannot simply be folded into the main
// tsconfig: Next builds from that one, and pulling ~200 node-side scripts into
// the app's program changes what the build compiles. Kept separate, run by its
// own guard (scripts/check-scripts-types.ts).
//
// `include` is scripts/** ONLY, and that is load-bearing. It first read
// ["scripts/**", "src/**"], which pulled the Payload admin routes into the
// program — and those import `importMap.js`, a file Payload GENERATES and
// .gitignore excludes. It exists in a working tree and not in a fresh
// checkout, so the guard passed locally and failed in CI with three TS2307s,
// and the baseline had been recorded in an environment the check never runs
// in. A guard whose answer depends on files git does not carry is measuring
// the developer's laptop, not the repo.
//
// Narrowing costs nothing: tsc still follows every import out of scripts/ and
// type-checks what it finds, which is exactly this guard's scope. The rest of
// src/ is the main tsconfig's job and the build already runs it. Verified in a
// fresh clone with no generated files: the same 63 signatures, all under
// scripts/, byte-identical to the committed baseline.
{
	"extends": "./tsconfig.json",
	"include": ["scripts/**/*.ts"],
	"exclude": ["node_modules", "scout-mcp/**", "api-client/**"]
}
